// TEST: Nested Map Capture Unit Cancels
// ============================================================================
// The ARRAY sibling of units/052. There the cancelling product sat directly in
// a scalar transcendental call; here it feeds a NESTED elementwise map
// (`exp <@> (i * w * ts)`) inside the kernel body -- so the rejection came
// from the nested apply's OWN second pass, which runs while the ENCLOSING
// param `w` is still an unresolved infer var contributing no units. The
// element signature it judged was therefore provisional: exactly the
// dimensioned capture's own (`day`), even though 1/day * day cancels.
//
// This WAS a `REJECT-AT: codegen` probe, whose own header said "turn it into a
// value test when #3 lands". S2 landed it: the kernel body is emitted through
// the lifted `__lambda_N` (call-don't-inline), the kernel-local `let e` is
// FORCED at lowering rather than silently dropped, and the fused reduce over
// the hoisted broadcast operand no longer refuses. So the units claim is now
// checked the strong way -- by the arithmetic it licenses.
//
// WHAT THE VALUES ASSERT. `1/day * day` cancels to dimensionless AND the
// magnitudes are exact reciprocals, so `w * ts` passes through UNSCALED: with
// ws = [1, 2] and ts = [1, 2, 3] the exponent is literally w*t, not
// 86400*w*t. outC is sum_t e^{i w t} and outR is sum_t e^{w t}; a magnitude
// slip of 86400 would be visible in the first digit. units/074 pins the same
// arithmetic in the top-level spelling, and units/073 pins the sin twin.
//
// The residue twins that must still REJECT (at lower, BL3006) are
// unit-errors/004 (real) and unit-errors/005 (complex): if the unit walk ever
// regresses to rejecting the cancellation, this test fails at lower instead.
// ============================================================================
Unit second
Unit day = 86400 * second
let i = complex(0.0, 1.0)
let ts: Array<Float<day> like Idx<3>> = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]
let ws: Array<Float<1/day> like Idx<2>> = [1.0, 2.0]

// complex: the lsdft.blade shape (e^{i w t}) -- this used to hard-reject BL3006
let outC = ws <@> lambda(w) -> {
    let e = exp <@> (i * w * ts)
    reduce(e, (+))
} |> compute

// real: accepted before too, but only VACUOUSLY (the real broadcast never
// stamped a unit at all, so nothing was checked); now the units are actually
// computed and actually cancel.
let outR = ws <@> lambda(w) -> {
    let e = exp <@> (w * ts)
    reduce(e, (+))
} |> compute

// outC[k] = sum_{t in {1,2,3}} e^{i*ws[k]*t}
// EXPECT: outC = [(-0.865837027279448, 1.89188841969345), (-0.109620170760388, -0.126920566681172)]
// outR[k] = sum_{t in {1,2,3}} e^{ws[k]*t}   (e+e^2+e^3, e^2+e^4+e^6)
// EXPECT: outR = [30.1928748505774, 465.41599962481]
